  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Low-Frequency Behavior",
    "lesson_core_move": "Evaluate low-frequency behavior by examining $H(j\\omega)$ as $\\omega$ approaches 0 and interpreting its magnitude and phase to decide how an LTI system scales, shifts, passes, or attenuates slowly varying inputs.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Low-Frequency Behavior",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Low-frequency behavior means asking what the frequency response does near DC, so we examine $H(j\\omega)$ as $\\omega \\to 0$. For slowly varying inputs, that neighborhood tells us the system's effective scaling and phase shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\omega \\to 0} H(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The value at $\\omega = 0$, written $H(j0)$, is the DC reference point. Its magnitude gives the low-frequency gain, and its angle gives the low-frequency phase shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the simplest base case, if $H(j\\omega)=K$ near $\\omega=0$, then a slow sinusoid $A\\cos(\\omega_0 t + \\phi)$ comes out approximately multiplied by $|K|$ and shifted by $\\angle K$. If $K$ is negative, that phase shift includes an inversion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) \\approx |H(j0)|A\\cos(\\omega_0 t + \\phi + \\angle H(j0))",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the rule for the rest of the topic is fixed: read the limit at $\\omega=0$, then interpret magnitude as scaling and phase as the shift applied to the slow part of the input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Low-Frequency Limit of $H(j\\omega)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{2-j\\omega}{4+j\\omega}$, evaluate $\\lim_{\\omega\\to 0}H(j\\omega)$ and state the low-frequency gain and phase.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the low-frequency limit, so we evaluate the transfer function at $\\omega=0$. Because this rational expression is continuous there, direct substitution gives the DC value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\omega \\to 0} H(j\\omega) &= \\lim_{\\omega \\to 0} \\frac{2 - j\\omega}{4 + j\\omega} \\\\ &= \\frac{2 - j0}{4 + j0} \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $H(j0)=\\frac{1}{2}$. The low-frequency gain is $\\frac{1}{2}$, and the low-frequency phase is $0$ because the limit is a positive real number.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j0)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\qquad |H(j0)|=\\frac{1}{2}, \\qquad \\angle H(j0)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means slowly varying input components are halved with no phase shift. For a slow sinusoid $A\\cos(\\omega_0 t + \\phi)$, the output is approximately $\\frac{1}{2}A\\cos(\\omega_0 t + \\phi)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) \\approx \\frac{1}{2}A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Low-Frequency Magnitude Scaling",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Suppose $H(j0)=\\frac{1}{2}$. For a slowly varying sinusoidal input, decide whether the system attenuates, passes, or amplifies the component and by what factor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the low-frequency gain, so we read the magnitude of $H(j0)$ directly. Here $|H(j0)|=\\frac{1}{2}$, and the key comparison is with 1.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j0)| = \\frac{1}{2} < 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the magnitude is less than 1, the system attenuates the slowly varying sinusoidal component. The output amplitude is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) \\approx \\frac{1}{2}A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the low-frequency magnitude scaling factor is $\\frac{1}{2}$, and the classification is attenuation. In general, values below 1 attenuate, values equal to 1 pass, and values above 1 amplify.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Low-Frequency Phase Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Suppose $H(j0)=-2$. For a slowly varying sinusoidal input, determine the low-frequency phase shift and describe the resulting output component.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the low-frequency gain, and here the key feature is the negative sign in $H(j0)=-2$. A negative real constant has angle $\\pi$, so the phase shift is an inversion rather than a delay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j0)=-2=2e^{j\\pi}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The magnitude is 2, so the slow sinusoid is doubled in amplitude, and the phase adds $\\pi$. That means the output keeps the same frequency but flips sign relative to the input.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) \\approx 2A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi+\\pi) = -2A\\cos(\\omega_0 t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the low-frequency phase shift is $\\pi$: the component is inverted while its amplitude is scaled by 2. The sign of $H(j0)$ gives the phase classification directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Frequency Behavior",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Deciding How Slowly Varying Inputs Change Through the System",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $H(j0)=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x(t)=A\\cos(0.02t+\\phi)$, describe the approximate output for this slowly varying component.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the low-frequency limit, so we read the slow sinusoid through the constant value $H(j0)=-\\frac{3}{2}$. The magnitude gives the scaling and the negative sign gives a phase shift of $\\pi$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j0)=-\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}e^{j\\pi}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply both parts together: multiply the amplitude by $\\frac{3}{2}$ and add $\\pi$ to the sinusoid's phase. The output keeps the same slow frequency, but it is scaled up and inverted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) \\approx \\frac{3}{2}A\\cos(0.02t+\\phi+\\pi) = -\\frac{3}{2}A\\cos(0.02t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the slowly varying input is amplified by $1.5$ and shifted by $\\pi$. The combined low-frequency rule is to use the magnitude for scale and the sign for inversion.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
